---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';
---

<Page
	title="About Us"
	description="ProofNews is an independent fact-checking publication investigating viral claims, health myths, and tech rumors — and showing its work."
	eyebrow="Who We Are"
>
	<p>
		<strong>ProofNews</strong> is an independent fact-checking publication with a simple rule:
		don't share it until we check it. Every day, millions of claims race around social media —
		a miracle cure in a forwarded message, a terrifying headline about your phone, a photograph
		that is not what its caption says it is. Most of them are shared by people acting in good
		faith who simply never saw the claim checked. ProofNews exists to do that checking in
		public: we take the claims people are actually sharing, trace each one back to where it
		came from, weigh it against the best available evidence, and publish a clear verdict with
		the evidence attached.
	</p>
	<p>
		We are not a news wire and we do not chase breaking news. Our subject is the claim itself —
		where it started, how it mutated as it spread, and whether what it asserts survives contact
		with primary sources: peer-reviewed studies, official data, and the direct statements of
		the institutions involved. When the evidence is genuinely unsettled, we say so. A verdict
		of Unproven is a legitimate answer, and we would rather publish it than force a certainty
		the research does not support.
	</p>

	<h2>What We Check</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Health</strong> — wellness myths, diet claims, miracle cures, and medical rumors,
			checked against real research rather than testimonials.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Technology</strong> — AI scares, gadget rumors, privacy panics, and digital-age
			urban legends.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Science</strong> — space myths, animal folklore, science misconceptions, and bad
			pop science.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Viral Claims</strong> — whatever is trending right now: the forwarded warning,
			the too-perfect statistic, the story that sounds made up because it is.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>How an Investigation Works</h2>
	<p>Every fact-check on ProofNews follows the same five steps:</p>
	<ol>
		<li>
			<strong>Claim selection.</strong> We choose claims that are widely shared, consequential
			if believed, and checkable — a claim has to make a factual assertion that evidence can
			confirm or refute.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Source tracing.</strong> We track the claim to its origin: the original study,
			post, broadcast, or announcement it grew from, which is often very different from the
			version now circulating.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Evidence gathering.</strong> We consult the strongest sources available —
			peer-reviewed research and meta-analyses first, then official guidance from bodies such
			as the WHO, CDC, FDA, and NASA, then reputable reporting.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Review before publication.</strong> Before anything is published, the draft is
			reviewed against its own sources: every figure, date, and quotation is checked back
			against the material it came from, and the verdict is tested against the evidence one
			final time.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Verdict and sources.</strong> We publish the verdict with the reasoning shown,
			and every new fact-check ends with a Sources section linking the primary material so
			you can verify the verdict yourself.
		</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>How We Rate Claims</h2>
	<p>Every investigation ends with one of five verdicts:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>True</strong> — the claim is accurate as stated.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Mostly True</strong> — the claim is accurate overall, with minor caveats or
			missing context that does not change its substance.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Misleading</strong> — the claim contains real facts but arranges, exaggerates,
			or strips them of context so that a reasonable reader takes away something untrue.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>False</strong> — the claim is not supported by the evidence, or is directly
			contradicted by it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Unproven</strong> — the available evidence is not enough to confirm or refute
			the claim either way.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The evidence decides the verdict — never the other way around. If new research overturns
		the evidence behind a published verdict, we update the article and say so openly, under the
		rules on our <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a> page.
	</p>

	<h2>Our Editorial Standards</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Evidence first.</strong> We base verdicts on primary sources — peer-reviewed
			research, official data, and direct statements — wherever possible, and we rank a
			meta-analysis above a single small study, and both above an anecdote.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Show our work.</strong> We explain where every key fact came from and link our
			sources, so the checking itself can be checked.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Corrections.</strong> When we get something wrong, we correct it promptly and
			transparently. If you spot an error, please <a href="/contact">let us know</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>No false balance.</strong> We follow the weight of the evidence — we don't give
			equal airtime to fringe positions the data doesn't support.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Independence.</strong> ProofNews is independently published and advertising
			supported. Advertisers and sponsors have no say in which claims we check or which
			verdicts we reach. Our full standards are published on our
			<a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial Policy</a> page.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>How Our Content Is Made</h2>
	<p>
		ProofNews articles are researched and drafted with the help of modern research tools, and
		every fact-check passes a pre-publication review against its sources before it goes live:
		claims, figures, and quotations are verified against the material they cite, and the
		verdict must match that evidence. We do not publish invented experts, fabricated
		quotations, or sources that do not exist — if a fact cannot be traced to a real source, it
		does not go in. Fact-checking is published under the ProofNews name as an organisation,
		because the verdict belongs to the evidence, not to a personality.
	</p>

	<h2>Images</h2>
	<p>
		Photography on ProofNews comes from <a href="https://unsplash.com" rel="noopener">Unsplash</a>,
		unless otherwise credited. Images are illustrative and may not depict the specific claim or
		event described in an article.
	</p>

	<h2>Get in Touch</h2>
	<p>
		Seen a claim we should check, or found a mistake in one of our verdicts? We'd love to hear
		from you — head over to our <a href="/contact">contact page</a> or email us directly at
		<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
	</p>
</Page>
